# Caddyfile for the AWOW always-on core.
#
# Split-horizon: the SAME hostnames resolve to this box's LAN IP at home
# (Technitium) and to the public IP away (public DNS + router forward). Because
# the hostname never changes, the public cert Caddy obtains via ACME validates on
# the LAN too — no cert warnings, no internal hostname.
#
# AUTH MODEL (WI-10.2, decisions D1/D2):
#   - tracker.<domain>  -> oauth2-proxy (Google sign-in). NO basic_auth on this
#     host; oauth2-proxy is the gate and forwards verified identity headers
#     (X-Forwarded-Email / X-Forwarded-User) to the tracker container.
#   - actual.<domain>, dns.<domain>, map.<domain> -> KEEP basic_auth (the Owner's
#     single-user services; oauth2-proxy is only for the multi-user tracker).
#
#   - {$WALL_HOST}:{$WALL_PORT} -> the office-wall kiosk site (SR-016). A THIRD
#     auth model: no interactive login at all, identity injected by source IP.
#     Read the block's own banner before touching it — it is security-critical.
#
# Env vars (set in docker-compose.yml from .env):
#   {$DOMAIN} {$LAN_IP} {$TRACKER_HOST} {$ACTUAL_HOST} {$ACME_EMAIL} {$MAIN_BOX_IP}
#   {$ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_USER} {$ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_HASH}
#   {$DNS_BASICAUTH_USER} {$DNS_BASICAUTH_HASH}
#   {$WALL_HOST} {$WALL_PORT} {$PANEL_IP} {$PANEL_USER_SUB}
#   {$GAME_HOST} {$GAME_WS_SECRET}

{
    # WHERE rate_limit SITS IN THE CHAIN, and it is the whole point of the
    # directive. `rate_limit` is a plugin handler, so it has no place in the
    # built-in directive order and would otherwise run AFTER basic_auth - i.e.
    # only for callers who already authenticated, which is precisely backwards
    # for a brute-force guard. Before, not after.
    #
    # IF CADDY REFUSES TO LOAD NAMING THIS LINE, the directive was renamed;
    # `basic_auth` is the 2.7+ spelling and `basicauth` the old alias. Validate
    # with `caddy validate` before reloading - a bad global block takes down
    # every site at once, which is the failure this file already documents.
    order rate_limit before basic_auth

    # NO HTTP/3, and this is a correctness fix rather than a preference.
    #
    # Caddy serves h3 by default and advertises it on EVERY response:
    #   alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=2592000
    # That header is a 30-day promise that this box answers QUIC on UDP 443.
    # docker-compose publishes `443:443` — TCP ONLY — so nothing on the host has
    # ever listened on UDP 443 and the promise was false the moment it shipped.
    #
    # WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE, because the symptom points nowhere near the cause. A
    # browser believes the header, caches it, and on its NEXT visit tries QUIC
    # first: the packets go to a port nobody holds, and the tab sits until it
    # gives up with ERR_CONNECTION_TIMED_OUT. curl is unaffected (HTTP/1.1, no
    # alt-svc cache), so the box tests healthy from every script while the Owner
    # cannot open the site — measured 2026-09-08 on tracker.<domain>, where curl
    # returned 302 from the same PC whose Edge timed out. Every host on this
    # Caddy shares the fault, not just the tracker.
    #
    # WHY DISABLE RATHER THAN PUBLISH UDP 443. Publishing it fixes the LAN only.
    # The alt-svc header goes to WAN clients too, and the router forwards no UDP
    # — so an external browser would keep timing out for another 30 days. Making
    # the advertisement FALSE everywhere is the fix that matches reality on both
    # sides of the router. If h3 is wanted later it needs BOTH a `443:443/udp`
    # publish and a router UDP forward, and then this line comes out.
    servers {
        protocols h1 h2
    }

    # ACME account email for public certs (Let's Encrypt / ZeroSSL).
    email {$ACME_EMAIL}

    # dns-01, NOT http-01 — CHANGED 2026-08-08, and it is a correctness fix
    # rather than a preference.
    #
    # The default challenge needs Let's Encrypt to reach this box on :80 from the
    # public internet. It could not, so NOTHING was ever issued and Caddy served
    # no certificate at all — a TLS handshake that dies with `internal error`,
    # which is why the verification run saw HTTP 000 from the tracker, Actual,
    # the DNS console, the apex AND the kiosk site simultaneously. One missing
    # router forward, every HTTPS interface down, and no fallback: Caddy will not
    # quietly drop to its internal CA.
    #
    # The kiosk site is the part that makes this a design fault and not just a
    # firewall gap. It is LAN-only by ratification (OI-12) — published on LAN_IP,
    # /32 allow-list, 403 default — and it still could not get a certificate
    # without a WAN path. The panel's entire surface hung off the internet.
    #
    # dns-01 proves domain control by writing a TXT record through Cloudflare's
    # API. Nothing has to reach this box, it works for names that resolve to a
    # private address (which split-horizon makes all of these on the LAN), and it
    # can issue for the apex and a wildcard alike.
    #
    # NEEDS THE PLUGIN, which is why stack/caddy/Dockerfile exists: Caddy's DNS
    # providers are compiled in and the stock image has none. The token is the
    # same Zone:DNS:Edit one the ddns updater already uses.
    #
    # THE ISSUER IS CONFIGURED PER SITE, NOT HERE, and that is not a style
    # choice — see the (acme_cloudflare) snippet below. A global
    # `acme_dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}` works and was what shipped
    # first, but it takes no `resolvers`, and without those the challenge can
    # never complete on this box. The block form that would carry them
    # (`acme_dns cloudflare <token> { resolvers ... }`) does not adapt at all —
    # measured, not assumed.
}

# ── ACME dns-01 against Cloudflare, with PUBLIC resolvers ────────────────────
# ADDED 2026-08-08, after the challenge hung on a live hub with no error at all.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS WITHOUT `resolvers`. Caddy writes the _acme-challenge TXT record
# through the Cloudflare API — that part works, and was verified at Cloudflare —
# and then polls DNS until it can see the record itself before telling the CA to
# validate. That poll uses the system resolver, which on this box is Technitium:
# AUTHORITATIVE for {$DOMAIN} and holding no _acme-challenge record, so it does
# not forward the query, it answers NXDOMAIN. Authoritatively. Forever.
#
# The result is a challenge stuck at "trying to solve" with nothing logged as an
# error, no certificate, and every HTTPS site serving a TLS handshake failure.
#
# IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE ddns HEALTHCHECK (docker-compose.yml), found the
# same day: a component that must see the PUBLIC view of DNS, pointed by default
# at a resolver built to answer with the private one. Split-horizon is correct
# and so is each component; what is wrong is letting them share a resolver.
#
# WHY NOT JUST GIVE THE CONTAINER `dns: 1.1.1.1` the way ddns has? Because Caddy
# resolves its upstreams by CONTAINER NAME — `reverse_proxy tracker:8787`,
# `oauth2-proxy:4180`, `actual:5006` — and those only work through Docker's
# embedded resolver at 127.0.0.11. Overriding the container's DNS would fix the
# challenge and break every proxied site. This scopes the public resolvers to
# the ACME challenge and nothing else.
#
# IMPORTED BY EVERY SITE. The global acme_dns was deliberately removed rather
# than left as a fallback: a site that forgets this import now fails fast on
# http-01 (connection refused, in seconds) instead of hanging on a poll that
# cannot terminate. Fast and loud beats quiet and stuck.
(acme_cloudflare) {
    tls {
        dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
        # Public resolvers, deliberately NOT this box's.
        resolvers 1.1.1.1 1.0.0.1
        # Cloudflare publishes in seconds; the default 2m has no headroom for a
        # slow propagation and the failure it produces is indistinguishable from
        # the one above.
        propagation_timeout 5m
    }
}

# ── shared snippets: one basic_auth guard per single-user service ─────────────
# Caddy resolves {$VAR} at config-load, so a single (protect) snippet can't carry
# two different credential pairs; give each guarded host its own snippet.
#
# WHY THE PREFIX NAMES THE SITE AND NOT THE SERVER (the Owner asked, 2026-08-29:
# "is DNS_BASICAUTH_USER used for caddy?"). Yes - BOTH pairs are Caddy's own
# basic_auth, and neither is the credential of the thing behind it. The prefix
# names WHICH DOOR the pair opens, because there are two independent pairs and
# they have to stay distinguishable. Behind each one sits a SECOND, different
# credential that Caddy knows nothing about:
#
#   actual.<domain>  Caddy basic_auth ->  Actual's own server password
#                    (ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_*)  (FinanceActualPassword). Actual has no
#                                          concept of a username, which is why the
#                                          username field belongs to the BROWSER
#                                          dialog and not to Actual.
#   dns.<domain>     Caddy basic_auth ->  Technitium's admin login
#                    (DNS_BASICAUTH_*)     (TechnitiumAdminPassword)
#
(protect_actual) {
    basic_auth {
        {$ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_USER} {$ACTUAL_BASICAUTH_HASH}
    }
    import throttle
}
(protect_dns) {
    basic_auth {
        {$DNS_BASICAUTH_USER} {$DNS_BASICAUTH_HASH}
    }
    import throttle
}

# -- the throttle, and an honest account of what it does and does not buy ------
# ADDED 2026-08-29 (the Owner). Caddy's standard build ships no rate limiter, so
# this needs the caddy-ratelimit plugin compiled in - see stack/caddy/Dockerfile,
# which proves the module is present at BUILD time rather than discovering it is
# missing when Caddy refuses to load this block.
#
# 120 EVENTS A MINUTE IS NOT A BRUTE-FORCE LIMIT AND IS NOT PRETENDING TO BE.
# basic_auth guards the whole site, so EVERY request carries an auth attempt: one
# page load of Actual is dozens of them. A limit tight enough to stop guessing
# would break the app for the person who knows the password. What this stops is
# flooding and scraping; what stops GUESSING is fail2ban, which keys on 401s
# specifically and bans at the firewall, and the remote_ip gate below, which takes
# these two hosts off the internet altogether. Three layers, three jobs - and this
# is the only one of the three that still applies to a caller already on the LAN.
#
# key {remote_host} is the REAL peer address, matching the remote_ip discipline
# below. It must never become a header-derived value.
(throttle) {
    rate_limit {
        zone per_ip {
            key    {remote_host}
            events 120
            window 1m
        }
        # ── IPv6: RATE-LIMIT THE /64, NOT THE ADDRESS ────────────────────────
        # ADDED 2026-08-29 (adversarial review finding 4, gpt-5.6-sol).
        #
        # A per-ADDRESS bucket is close to meaningless against IPv6. The
        # smallest block a residential or hosted client is normally handed is a
        # /64 - eighteen quintillion addresses - and cycling source addresses
        # inside it hands the attacker a fresh 120/min bucket every time, at no
        # cost. The limit reads as tight and is trivially bypassed.
        #
        # It also cuts the other way, which is the part worth stating: Caddy
        # allocates rate-limit STATE per distinct key, so address-cycling grows
        # that state without bound - a memory-pressure problem for the whole
        # proxy, not just for the site being attacked.
        #
        # /64 is the correct granularity: it is the standard end-site subnet, so
        # it groups one client's addresses together without merging two
        # different customers. IPv4 keeps its /32 default - there, one address
        # really is one client.
        ipv6_prefix 64
    }
}

# -- the access log fail2ban reads ---------------------------------------------
# JSON, to a bind-mounted path the HOST can read (docker-compose.yml mounts
# /var/log/caddy). The fail2ban filter keys on status 401 plus request.remote_ip.
# Only the guarded sites import this: an access log of every kiosk asset would
# bury the handful of lines that matter.
(access_log) {
    log {
        output file /var/log/caddy/access.log {
            roll_size 10MiB
            roll_keep 5
        }
        format json
    }
}

# ── tracker.<domain> — the NagLight tracker (multi-user via oauth2-proxy) ──────
# D2: NO basic_auth here. Caddy hands the whole host to oauth2-proxy, which runs
# the Google OAuth flow and reverse-proxies to the tracker container on success,
# injecting the identity headers the tracker's trusted-header mode reads (D3).
# The /oauth2/* callback + sign-in routes live inside oauth2-proxy too.
{$TRACKER_HOST} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    encode zstd gzip
    reverse_proxy oauth2-proxy:4180 {
        # oauth2-proxy needs the original host/scheme to build its redirect URL.
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
    }
}

# SR-016 opt-in private boundary. Substitution happens before import parsing;
# an invalid selector refuses adaptation instead of falling back to legacy.
(wall_api_false) {
    handle /api/* {
        reverse_proxy tracker:8787 {
            # +---------------------------------------------------------+
            # | DO NOT ADD `header_up -X-Forwarded-User` HERE.          |
            # +---------------------------------------------------------+
            # It reads like the right thing (strip, then inject) and it is
            # how the design brief wrote it — but Caddy applies header ops
            # in a FIXED order regardless of the order you write them:
            # add -> set -> DELETE -> replace. A delete of the same field
            # therefore runs AFTER the set and erases the injected identity,
            # the tracker sees no identity at all, and every panel request
            # 403s. Found for real in the V1 sim, 2026-07-29 (the site failed
            # CLOSED, which is the correct direction, but it never works).
            #
            # The strip is not lost: `header_up <Name> <value>` is a SET, and
            # a set REPLACES every existing value of that field — a
            # client-supplied X-Forwarded-User (even repeated) cannot
            # survive it. That is a behaviour a reader cannot see, so it is
            # asserted with a forged header in validate-sim.sh check 7 rather
            # than trusted. X-Forwarded-Email has no injected value, so it
            # takes the plain delete.
            header_up -X-Forwarded-Email
            # The panel is the Owner (D-W7: full view, no per-view filter),
            # so panel check-offs appear as him.
            header_up X-Forwarded-User {$PANEL_USER_SUB}
        }
    }
}

(wall_api_true) {
    # The gateway owns session verification and upstream identity. This must
    # never retain the legacy identity injection or proxy arbitrary tracker APIs.
    handle /api/* {
        respond "wall: legacy API disabled" 403
    }
    handle /v1/* {
        reverse_proxy panel-access:8788
    }
}

# ── {$WALL_HOST}:{$WALL_PORT} — the office-wall kiosk site (SR-016, OI-12) ─────
# RATIFIED 2026-07-29 (the Owner; the belt-and-braces "LAN port + /32" variant).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the office wall panel is a keyboard-less ambient display, so it
# cannot complete an interactive Google consent — every session expiry would end
# with the office wall showing a Google login screen indefinitely. This site
# therefore BYPASSES oauth2-proxy and becomes a SECOND injector of the tracker's
# trusted identity headers. That is exactly why it needed ratification: IF-002's
# premise ("trusted headers only ever come from oauth2-proxy") now has a second
# source, and NagLight's trusted-header mode has no header HMAC in v1.
#
# FOUR THINGS MAKE IT SAFE. All four are load-bearing; do not "simplify" any of
# them without re-ratifying:
#   1. STRIP BEFORE SET. The `header_up -X-Forwarded-…` lines come FIRST so any
#      client-supplied identity header is DELETED before ours is injected.
#      Asserted with a forged header in the V1 sim (validate-sim.sh check 7) —
#      Caddy's defaults are not trusted for this.
#   2. /32, NOT THE LAN CIDR. "anything on the LAN is the Owner" is far weaker
#      than it sounds once guest Wi-Fi, IoT gear, and a Minecraft server that
#      accepts inbound connections share the network. The panel's DHCP
#      reservation (on its hardware MAC — see the wall autoinstall) is what makes
#      a single-address match enforceable. `remote_ip` matches the REAL peer
#      address; never switch it to `client_ip`, which trusts X-Forwarded-For and
#      would hand the allow-list to any client willing to forge a header.
#   3. A LAN-ONLY PORT THE ROUTER NEVER FORWARDS ({$WALL_PORT}). The name
#      resolves publicly (the DDNS wildcard), so structural unreachability is
#      worth more than a rule: the router forwards :80/:443 only, and compose
#      publishes this port bound to {$LAN_IP} — not 0.0.0.0. Two independent
#      reasons a WAN packet cannot arrive. **Do NOT add a port-forward for
#      {$WALL_PORT} on the router.**
#   4. THE 403 DEFAULT. Anything that is not the panel — LAN or WAN — gets 403
#      before it can reach a handler. The distinct body makes the sim able to
#      tell Caddy's 403 apart from the tracker's own no-identity 403.
#
# CERTIFICATE: see stack/README.md §10 — the name is publicly resolvable via the
# DDNS wildcard, and Caddy solves the ACME challenge over the EXISTING :80/:443
# listeners, not over {$WALL_PORT}.
{$WALL_HOST}:{$WALL_PORT} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    encode zstd gzip

    # The one address allowed to be the panel. /32 = exactly one host.
    @panel remote_ip {$PANEL_IP}/32

    handle @panel {
        # ---- the tracker API, same-origin (OfficeWallNaglight needs doc §3.2) --
        # NagLight sends no CORS headers, so the shell's fetch("/api/today") only
        # works if the shell and the API share an origin. This site is that origin.
        import wall_api_{$PANEL_ACCESS_ENABLED:false}

        # ---- the shell's static build ------------------------------------------
        # Payload dir the IMAGE populates from the OfficeWallNaglight built
        # artifact (IF-005 — the packaging contract is still Planned, so the dir
        # ships with a README and nothing else). Bind-mounted read-only into the
        # caddy container; see docker-compose.yml.
        handle {
            root * /srv/wall-shell
            file_server
        }

        # ---- OPTIONAL: Subsonic streaming (Navidrome tier-2 profile) -----------
        # The panel plays music from its own synced copy (D-W8 rider), so this is
        # opt-in extra, not a dependency. Enabling it is the usual tier-2 dance:
        # add `navidrome` to COMPOSE_PROFILES, then uncomment. handle_path strips
        # the prefix so the shell's relative `SUBSONIC.url=/music` reaches
        # Navidrome's /rest/* API with no hostname baked into the app.
        # handle_path /music/* {
        #     reverse_proxy navidrome:4533
        # }
        #
        # ---- /media/* is PANEL-LOCAL by ruling (OI-15, 2026-07-29) — no route here.
    }

    # Everything else — including every WAN packet if one ever arrives. A second
    # `handle` (not a bare `respond`) so the mutual exclusion is explicit and
    # cannot depend on directive-ordering subtleties.
    handle {
        respond "wall: panel only" 403
    }
}

# ── actual.<domain> — Actual Budget (single-user; KEEP basic_auth) ─────────────
{$ACTUAL_HOST} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    import access_log
    encode zstd gzip

    # LAN/VPN ONLY (the Owner, 2026-08-29: "ideally actual only answers from the
    # local network or vpn"). The router forwards :80/:443 to this box, so before
    # this block Actual answered an unlimited-guess basic_auth prompt to the whole
    # internet - in front of budget data and, once linked, a SimpleFIN bank-sync
    # credential.
    #
    # THIS DOES NOT COST A CERTIFICATE, which is what makes it safe to do:
    # issuance is dns-01 through Cloudflare, so Let's Encrypt never needs to reach
    # this box. The kiosk site has been LAN-only WITH a public cert since OI-12;
    # this is the same arrangement, one host over.
    #
    # remote_ip, NEVER client_ip - the same rule the wall block states at length.
    # client_ip trusts X-Forwarded-For and would hand the allow-list to anyone
    # willing to forge a header. Caddy is the edge here; remote_ip is the truth.
    #
    # LAN_CIDR defaults to `private_ranges` (every RFC1918 block), which is the
    # honest expression of "LAN or VPN, not the internet" and will cover a
    # WireGuard subnet on the day D5 lands without another edit. Narrowing it
    # answers a DIFFERENT question - which devices ON the LAN - and basic_auth is
    # what answers that one.
    @lan remote_ip {$LAN_CIDR}
    handle @lan {
        import protect_actual
        reverse_proxy actual:5006
    }
    # A WAN caller is refused BEFORE basic_auth, so it is never even told there is
    # a prompt here. A second `handle` rather than a bare `respond`, so the mutual
    # exclusion cannot depend on directive-ordering subtleties.
    handle {
        respond "actual: LAN only" 403
    }
}

# ── dns.<domain> — Technitium web console (single-admin; KEEP basic_auth) ──────
# Technitium binds :5380 on the host (host-network); proxy to the host gateway.
dns.{$DOMAIN} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    import access_log
    encode zstd gzip

    # LAN/VPN ONLY, for the same reasons as actual.<domain> above - and arguably
    # more so: this console can rewrite every name the household resolves.
    @lan remote_ip {$LAN_CIDR}
    handle @lan {
        import protect_dns
        # Technitium binds :5380 on the HOST (host-network), so this crosses back
        # out of the bridge rather than naming a compose service.
        reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:5380
    }
    handle {
        respond "dns console: LAN only" 403
    }
}

# ── {$GAME_HOST} — the Gun Master 3 crossplay relay (profile `gunmaster3`) ────
# ADDED 2026-08-29 (the Owner). Inert until the profile is on: with the service
# down the upstream name does not resolve and this host 502s, which is the
# correct shape for an opt-in site.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE SITE ON THIS BOX THAT DELIBERATELY ANSWERS THE INTERNET WITH
# NO IDENTITY CHECK, and the reason is the requirement itself. The Owner wants to
# play with his son while away from home and NOT on the VPN — which is precisely
# the case the @lan remote_ip gate on actual.<domain> and dns.<domain> exists to
# refuse. Those two are right to be LAN-only; this one cannot be.
#
# ── THE DOOR IS A SECRET PATH, AND HERE IS WHAT THAT IS WORTH ────────────────
#
# The upgrade is accepted at /ws-{$GAME_WS_SECRET} (43 base64url chars = 32
# random bytes, T3-Generated, minted
# and never typed) and nowhere else. Be clear-eyed about the grade: this is a
# BEARER TOKEN IN A URL. It identifies nobody, it is equally valid from any
# address on earth, and anyone who ever sees it holds it until it is rotated.
# Treat the URL as a password — if it leaks, change GAME_WS_SECRET, re-materialise
# and re-paste it on each device; that is a complete revocation and it is cheap.
#
# WHY NOT basic_auth, which is this file's convention for single-user services:
# the browser's WebSocket constructor CANNOT SET REQUEST HEADERS. There is
# physically nowhere to put Authorization. The only browser route is
# wss://user:pass@host/ws, which neither Chrome nor Firefox specifies and which
# would put the password in the same box a nine-year-old types into. A convention
# that cannot be implemented is not a convention.
#
# WHY NOT oauth2-proxy, which WOULD genuinely authenticate the socket (the
# session cookie rides the same-origin upgrade): it needs a Google account for
# the son and a new redirect URI registered in Google Cloud Console by hand. It
# is the stronger answer and it is recorded as the follow-up, not dismissed.
#
# WHY THE 404 AND NOT A 403. The actual./dns. gates answer 403 because something
# is there and the caller may not have it. Here the honest answer is that nothing
# is at that path. It also keeps this host clear of fail2ban: that jail's filter
# matches 401 ONLY — deliberately, because banning on a gate's 403 could have
# locked the Owner out of the tracker from outside — and a family member
# mistyping a 32-character path must never be able to ban the address they are
# trying to play from.
#
# THE THROTTLE EARNS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS FILE. On actual. every
# page asset carries an auth attempt, so 120/min is flood control and that block
# says so. A WebSocket is ONE request that then stays open, so here 120/min is
# 120 CONNECTION ATTEMPTS per IP per minute — a genuinely tight limit, and the
# thing that caps the relay's one real DoS vector: sockets that connect and never
# send `hello` are held 5s and are NOT counted against MAX_PLAYERS.
#
# NO /health ROUTE, ON PURPOSE. The relay answers /health with
# access-control-allow-origin:* — harmless counters, but it would confirm to any
# scanner that a relay lives at this name. The container healthcheck probes it on
# 127.0.0.1 from the inside, so publishing it buys nothing.
#
# COSTS NO CERTIFICATE, same as the actual. block: issuance is dns-01 through
# Cloudflare, so Let's Encrypt never needs to reach this box.
#
# THE URL THE FAMILY PASTES (Multiplayer box on the game's Home screen):
#     wss://<GAME_HOST>/ws-<GAME_WS_SECRET>
# It needs NO game-client change — normalizeServerUrl() round-trips the path
# verbatim, and connect() only fills in /ws when the path is empty. Verified by
# reading FinnsGame's src/systems/{persistence,multiplayer}.ts, not assumed.
{$GAME_HOST} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    import throttle
    encode zstd gzip

    # ── AN EXACT, CASE-SENSITIVE MATCH — AND THE FIRST VERSION WAS NEITHER ───
    # CHANGED 2026-08-29 (adversarial review finding 5, gpt-5.6-sol), after this
    # block was measured against a live box rather than read:
    #
    #   * `path` MATCHERS IN CADDY ARE CASE-INSENSITIVE. With the secret typed
    #     in flipped case, the handshake still returned 101. base64url draws on
    #     64 symbols but only 38 survive case-folding, so the door was quietly
    #     narrower than the token looked. (Still ~226 bits, so this was never a
    #     practical break — it was a DOCUMENTATION LIE, which is worse in a file
    #     whose whole job is to be trusted about what it enforces.)
    #   * THE TRAILING `*` IS A PREFIX WILDCARD, not "the rest of one segment".
    #     `/ws-<secret>-anything` and `/ws-<secret>/deeper` both returned 101
    #     too. The old comment claimed the relay accepts exactly one path; it
    #     accepted an infinite family of them.
    #
    # `path_regexp` is Go's regexp: case-SENSITIVE, and `^…$` anchors both ends,
    # so exactly one path matches and the comment above is now true. The secret
    # is base64url ([A-Za-z0-9_-]) and carries no regexp metacharacter, so
    # substituting it here is safe — do NOT relax the alphabet without revisiting
    # that, because a `.` or `+` in the secret would widen this matcher silently.
    #
    # handle_path is gone with it: it strips a matched PREFIX, which only made
    # sense while this was a prefix match. An explicit rewrite to /ws — the one
    # path the relay accepts — replaces it, and any other path reaching the relay
    # would be answered with its own 400 rather than half-working.
    @ws path_regexp ^/ws-{$GAME_WS_SECRET}$
    handle @ws {
        rewrite * /ws
        reverse_proxy gunmaster3-relay:8367
    }

    # Everything else, including a bare / and a wrong secret. A second `handle`
    # rather than a bare `respond`, so the mutual exclusion cannot depend on
    # directive-ordering subtleties — the same discipline as the wall block.
    handle {
        respond "not found" 404
    }
}

# ── main-box services (BlueMap / Minecraft map etc.) — COMMENTED, opt-in ───────
# A flaky MAIN box may host the heavy services. Uncomment + set MAIN_BOX_IP in
# .env to have Caddy on THIS box reverse-proxy across the LAN to them. The
# provisioning script already creates a `map.<domain>` split-horizon record when
# MAIN_BOX_IP is set, so this just wires the proxy. KEEP basic_auth on these.
#
# map.{$DOMAIN} {
#     import protect_dns
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy {$MAIN_BOX_IP}:8100   # BlueMap default web port
# }

# ── tier-2 opt-in public sites (SR-012) — COMMENTED, uncomment per service ─────
# Publishing a tier-2 service is a deliberate THREE-step opt-in:
#   1. enable its compose profile (COMPOSE_PROFILES in .env),
#   2. uncomment its block here,
#   3. add its label to EXTRA_SUBDOMAINS in .env (split-horizon record).
# Every service below carries its OWN login — do NOT wrap them in basic_auth;
# their mobile/desktop clients authenticate against the app's API directly and
# a second auth layer breaks them. Most are also LAN-reachable on their LAN_IP
# port bind (exception: Vaultwarden, which is HTTPS-only by design — this site
# IS its ingress).
#
# vault.{$DOMAIN} {          # Vaultwarden — the Bitwarden clients point here
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy vaultwarden:80
# }
# photos.{$DOMAIN} {         # Immich — the mobile app needs this for away-from-home auto-backup
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy immich-server:2283
# }
# prism.{$DOMAIN} {          # PhotoPrism
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy photoprism:2342
# }
# jellyfin.{$DOMAIN} {       # Jellyfin
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy jellyfin:8096
# }
# music.{$DOMAIN} {          # Navidrome — Subsonic clients point here
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy navidrome:4533
# }
# audio.{$DOMAIN} {          # Audiobookshelf — its apps point here
#     encode zstd gzip
#     reverse_proxy audiobookshelf:80
# }
# home.{$DOMAIN} {           # Home Assistant — host-network, so proxy to the host.
#     encode zstd gzip       # HA rejects proxied requests until Caddy is listed in
#     reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:8123    # trusted_proxies in its config.
# }

# ── NO WILDCARD CERTIFICATE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED ───────
# A `*.{$DOMAIN}` site was added here 2026-08-29 and REVERTED the same evening.
# The goal was worth having: with one exact certificate per site, all six private
# hostnames are enumerable through public certificate-transparency logs, and a
# reflash re-issues all six against Let's Encrypt's 5-duplicates-per-168h limit —
# which this repo already flags as a real constraint on lab runs.
#
# IT DID NOT WORK, and the failure is worth recording so nobody re-attempts it
# the same way. Caddy 2.11.4 will SERVE a wildcard for a covered subdomain — every
# site here was measured serving `CN=*.{$DOMAIN}` — but it goes on MANAGING every
# exact name that has its own site block. The admin API showed all seven subjects
# under one automation policy, so a fresh box would obtain SEVEN certificates
# rather than two: strictly worse than the six it replaced, for no benefit.
# `prefer_wildcard` does not exist in this version — tried as a global option, a
# `tls` subdirective and an `auto_https` argument; all three are rejected.
#
# THE ONLY ARRANGEMENT THAT ACTUALLY GETS TWO is one `*.{$DOMAIN}` site block
# with `@host` matchers replacing the per-name blocks — which moves the routing
# of every guarded site, the oauth2-proxy handoff and the host:port kiosk site in
# a single edit to the most security-critical file on the box. Not worth doing
# immediately before an image freeze; recorded as a candidate for when the
# routing is being touched anyway, or for a Caddy version that prefers wildcards
# at the management layer and not just the serving one.

# ── apex — bare domain answers on-LAN too ──────────────────────────────────────
{$DOMAIN} {
    import acme_cloudflare
    respond "AWOW always-on core. Try https://{$TRACKER_HOST}" 200
}
